## 🌿 Puṣpa–6 — English Contemplative Conclusion

Consciousness does not travel from bondage to liberation.

It illuminates the experience called bondage, and it illuminates the recognition called liberation, without undergoing a modification in itself.

This radically changes the meaning of spiritual practice.

We are not attempting to manufacture an immortal Self out of a mortal being.

We are examining the assumption that the ever-present light of experience was ever confined to the changing object called “me.”

The body changes.
Thought changes.
Pleasure and pain alternate.
Even the conviction “I am bound” may be replaced by “I am free.”

Yet that by which both convictions are known does not arrive with either one.

**Bondage is therefore not a chain fastened to Consciousness.
Liberation is not the breaking of a chain that actually belonged to It.**

It is recognition.

And when recognition becomes complete, Vāsiṣṭha takes us still further:

**Nothing has truly arisen; nothing has truly disappeared. All is the stainless expanse of Consciousness.**

That statement prepares our next flower.

For immediately a magnificent question arises:

### **If Chiti alone is, what is this vast world that appears before us?**

And Sarga 195 answers with one of its most beautiful images—

**“व्योम्नि स्वप्न इवोदितम्…”**

### **The world has arisen like a dream in the sky.**

That shall be our **🌺 Puṣpa–7: “स्वप्न का पर्वत कहाँ से आया? — ब्रह्म में जगत् का अनुत्पन्न आभास।”** 

**श्रीराम जय राम जय जय राम।**
**ॐ नमो नारायणाय।**
**हरिः ॐ तत्सत्।**

